 API: `GET /api/performance?instance=N&from=&to=[&part=]` - parts `meta`, `grid` and, per engine, `cpu`, `sessions`,
 `waits`, `db`, `io` (PostgreSQL) or `cpu`, `sessions`, `waits`, `db`, `io` (SQL Server).
 
+## Restarts, failovers and builds
+
+Every instance chart marks each restart, failover, crash restart and build change in its range, listed under the chart
+(`GET /api/frame/events`), and each one is an info finding for 24 hours: `instance restarted`, `instance failed over`,
+`crash restart`, `build changed`. They come from the reads pgvigil already makes: an instance's first read is no event.
+
+- SQL Server: the start time moved. On another machine (a failover cluster's physical node, else the machine name) a
+  failover; on the same machine a restart when the start moved by over 120 s and the new start is after the read before
+  (the server's own clock at that read); any other move on the same machine is the host's clock stepping, no event.
+- PostgreSQL: the postmaster's start moved. A new start after the read before is a restart; a start at or before it
+  is a failover (a node already up took over), whatever the server's address: a container or pod recreated on a new
+  address reads restart, and a promotion behind a virtual IP or over a Unix socket reads failover. "The read before" is
+  the server's own clock at that read. Two edges read restart, since the timeline is not read: a standby created and
+  promoted between two reads, and a managed high-availability failover whose standby runs no PostgreSQL until it takes
+  over (an Amazon RDS Multi-AZ DB instance, Cloud SQL HA); the event's words keep both addresses. The managed edge
+  stays until task 390b, which reads the provider's own failover events (RDS events, Cloud SQL operations) for an
+  instance with its cloud block; the provider's word then wins.
+- A crash restart (PostgreSQL 15 and later): the statistics were reset with the postmaster kept, the server's crash
+  recovery; a crash the server log already reports is one finding, the log's. On 14 and older only the server log's
+  finding names it.
+- A build change: SQL Server's product version moved between two reads; on PostgreSQL a row of its version history (a
+  patch, an upgrade, an engine version read from AWS).
+- An event is dated at the new start when that is after the read before, else at the read that saw it: a failover onto
+  a node up for days is dated when it was seen, not days back. Events are kept 365 days.
+
 ## Not proven
 
 The SQL Server columns and charts on SQL Server 2016 (task 164), on Windows (task 144), on Amazon RDS for SQL Server (task 165) and on Azure SQL Managed Instance: `scripts/proof-mssql-perf.sh` runs on SQL Server 2017 to 2025 in Linux containers (through `scripts/proof-mssql-versions.sh`).
